Z||000000||86SEFIERO||06-15-2000||03:36 AM||||<BR>Which combo do you think would be better?||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||06-15-2000||07:16 AM||||I think you can build up a pre-88 Fiero with equally good if not better brakes and suspension. Just takes a little planning, work, and money. Since the 88 Fieros tend to cost more to buy anyway, you'll probably spend close to the same amount of cash.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||06-15-2000||08:15 AM||||My car soon to be GT Bastards car will hold over a 1g on a skid pad at Road Atlanta (1.13g to be exact) in both directions before it spills. 86 SE V6 4spd... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||06-15-2000||10:30 AM||||It all changes when you put on some good,sticky tires,and start to push it a bit.<P>With the stock tires,the '88 would be nicer because it would ride better,handle great,but with some newer technology rubber,the '88 would be twitching and jiggling over fast bumps,where the slightly firmer car would come into it's own.<BR>Guess it depends on how,and where you drive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||06-15-2000||11:25 AM||||My 88GT handles great with A509s which are about the stickiest tires you can get.  I never noticed any twitching or jiggling over fast bumps.  I'm sure you could make a pre 88 handle better than an 88, but you aren't going to do it with Koni shocks and a rear sway bar.  The 88s have more of a plush suspension which is great for a daily driver, but they also are better on the track.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula||06-15-2000||11:36 AM||||twitching and giggling????? NAW,<P>my 88 rides firm but it doesnt kill your behind.  and it stops 60-0 in about 100ft without locking up the brakes (it literally wont lock them up on pavement).<P>btw......it can take onramp turns at the posted speedlimit. 70 mph.....<P>I've driven 2 pre-88s and the ride was not as smooth and the handling felt similiar except for the turning radius, and also they were a lot harder to turn at low speeds.<P>all in all, I'd say spend the extra thousand and get an 88 if its going to be a daily driver, but if your going to race it and run it hard then you should get a pre-88 because it wont be so EXPENSIVE to replace parts.<P>This has been in my own opinion and if it offends anyone then calmly say that I'm wrong and please dont flame, because I didnt flame you.  Thanks, and have a nice day.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-15-2000||12:34 PM||||I think we stumbled onto the fact that the answer depends on how you drive. I agree, for a daily driver, you'll probably be better off with the 88. But for performance driving and/or occasional racing, the Koni coil-over suspension is far more tuneable and versatile.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||06-15-2000||02:00 PM||||Konis can't change suspension geometry.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||06-15-2000||05:42 PM||||You can get Koni's for an 88 fiero also you know!||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-15-2000||07:32 PM||||Someday I'll have a nice 88 GT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I hope.||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||06-15-2000||10:38 PM||||I haven't ridden in an 88 so i can't say my car rides better or worse... BUT my car will break your teeth when in race trim... (Koni set @ 7/8ths closed in rear 1/2 closed in front and rear bar full aginst the frame) But when driving back and forth to work a quick turn of the shiney white Koni dial and I've got a nice riding responsive yet, livable ride. <P>konis, eibachs, addco bars ALL poly bushings stock motor mounts. yokohama A032 (Race) and yokohama A509 (normal)  <P>OOOH tire rack has a DEAL on Yokohama AVS intermediates for our cars!! check it out... they are the same treadwear rating as the A509 but have a stiffer sidwall and a Z rating instead of an H. DON'T expect them to last! you want a sticky tire you live with replacing them!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> AND LOVE EVERY MINUTE OF IT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wannabe||06-15-2000||11:30 PM||||The geometry of the earlier suspension is simply not as good as the '88s. Spring an shock rates are easy to change on any car, but without completely changing the spindles, control arms and/or suspension mounting points, poor geometry can't be tuned out. In practice, you can probably get as much ultimate grip from the pre '88, but getting the most out of a car on the track depends mostly on driver confidence and being able to feel the approaching limits, especially when the road surface isn't perfectly smooth. This is the major benefit of improved geometry.||||reg||1
